Z||000006||Seanh||03-15-2001||12:33 AM||||Im sorry, I havent been on the forum much at all latly, been real busy. If you wanted to port an enclosure, you should use a bandpass enclosure. Run about a 23" x 4" port tube into the cabin. Check out <A HREF="http://www.subbox.com" TARGET=_blank>www.subbox.com</A>  for some picture's of it. I have one in my car. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Homicidal||03-15-2001||01:09 AM||||Did you do the port?  If so, how?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Seanh||03-15-2001||12:19 PM||||It comes with the port and equipment. For a DIY'er, use dryer tube. I cut the bottem of my spare tire well off, so the bottem of the subbox hangs out, but wont fall. Pull back the carpet on the inside passenger side, and cut yourself a 4" hole in the floor. Run the port tube in, and make a flange for it so it looks nice. Cut the same hole in your carpet and thats pretty much all there is to it. If you going to try it yourself, and dont have much stereo experience, makeing a bandpass box is not a very good idea. They are by far the most difficult to build and tune. Its 4th order bandpass by the way.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Homicidal||03-15-2001||08:37 PM||||I should clarify: <P>I already have a 10" Sub in the front, just an enclosed box, not ported or anything.<P>But I wanted to know was how to Port it and run a tube to the passenger footwell.  <P>Two things:<P>1) You cut the WHOLE bottom of the trunk space out?  Is there any way I can see a pic of this, because I am very confused.<P>2) Will mine sound good if it's ported?  The sub right now is firing towards the top of the hood/towards the cabin.||||reg||1
